# Data layer (D1 + Drizzle)

The database is **Cloudflare D1** (SQLite) everywhere — local dev and production both go through the `DB` binding. There is **no connection-string env var**; D1 is never a module singleton because it is only reachable from a request- or event-bound binding.

## Schema
`packages/db/src/schema.ts` (Drizzle `sqliteTable`) holds the Better Auth tables (`users`, `user_sessions`, `user_accounts`, `user_verifications` — with admin columns `role`, `banned`, `ban_reason`, `ban_expires`) the domain `documents` table (`r2_key`, `size`, `mime_type`, nullable `expires_at`, owner FK to `users`) and the two role tables, `roles` (`id` is the value of `users.role`) and `role_permissions` (what each role may do on a business resource, unique on role, resource and action, deleted with the role). Only the names of `businessStatement` (`packages/auth/src/permissions.ts`) are honored in `role_permissions`; see [Auth & RBAC](auth-rbac.md).

## Connecting
`packages/db/src/index.ts` exports `createDb(d1: D1Database)` → `drizzle(d1, { schema })`, plus the `Database` type and a re-export of `drizzle-orm` (so `eq`, `and`, `desc`, `lt`, `isNotNull`, … import from `@repo/db`).

- **Web (Server Components / Route Handlers):** call `getDb()` from `@/lib/server`, then `const db = await getDb()`. The seam (`apps/web/src/lib/env.ts` → `getEnv()`) reads the request's Cloudflare context and hands the `DB` binding to `createDb`.
- **tRPC procedures:** use `ctx.db` (the context builds it per request).
- **Server prefetch (Server Components):** a page that reads a tRPC query prefetches it with `prefetch(trpc.documents.list.queryOptions())` from `@/trpc/server` and wraps its client component in `<HydrateClient>`, as `apps/web/src/app/(dashboard)/page.tsx` and the admin users pages do. The page awaits `requireSession()` or `requireAdmin()` from `@/lib/auth/session` first: layouts and pages render in parallel, so without the guard an anonymous or non admin visit starts the protected query and logs its rejection. That path calls the router in process with the request headers, so the session and role permissions apply; a client component that only calls `useSuspenseQuery` fetches over HTTP during the server render, without the user's cookies, and fails with `UNAUTHORIZED`. A page for one record awaits it instead: `const user = await getQueryClient().fetchQuery(trpc.users.get.queryOptions({ id }))`, then `if (!user) notFound()` before `<HydrateClient>`, so an unknown id answers 404 with the not-found page. `users.get` returns `null` for it rather than throwing, since the server link logs a thrown error in development.
- **Cron Worker:** `import { createDb } from '@repo/db'`, then `const db = createDb(env.DB)` inside `scheduled(event, env, ctx)`.

## Paginated lists
`users.list` and `documents.list` return the rows of one page by offset, never the whole table, and the total is a separate procedure (`users.count`, `documents.count`). The URL is the source of truth: `?page=2&pageSize=25` on both lists, plus `&search=ada&sort=-name` on the users table. A parameter at its default (page 1, size 10, no search, newest first) is left out of the URL, and the back and forward buttons walk the pages.
- `packages/api/src/pagination.ts` owns the page rules and imports nothing server side: `PAGE_SIZES` (10, 25, 50, 100), `MAX_PAGE` (10,000, offset pagination slows down with depth), `pageInput` (the `.input` of a list), `parsePageParams` (the same page from a query string or a router) and `pageControls`. A missing or invalid `page` or `pageSize` is the default, so a hostile URL never reads an unbounded page or fails the request. `packages/api/src/user-list.ts` adds the search (capped at `MAX_SEARCH_LENGTH` characters) and the sort of the users table; `USER_SORTS` is the only way a URL picks a column to order by. Client components import these two files (`@repo/api/pagination`, `@repo/api/user-list`), never the router entry.
- `packages/api/src/page-query.ts` is the server half: `orderedPage(query, order, params)` applies the order, `limit` and `offset`, and `newestFirstPage` orders by `created_at` then `id`, so two rows created in the same second never repeat or skip across a page boundary. The users router maps each sort of `USER_SORTS` to its order (`ORDER_BY_SORT`); a sort without an entry fails `tsc`.
- The search is a case-insensitive substring of the name, the email or the role (`instr(lower(column), lower(text))`: the text is plain text, so `%` and `_` match themselves). It cannot use an index, so it scans the users table; the sort orders do not sort the matches again, they read an index.
- Indexes (`packages/db/src/schema.ts`) match the orders offered: `users (created_at, id)` for newest first, `users (name, id)` for both name orders, the unique email for both email orders, `documents (created_at, id)` for an admin or manager and `documents (user_id, created_at, id)` for the documents a user lists. A new sort needs its own index.
- The count is keyed by the search alone, and the tables give it a `staleTime`, so changing the page, the page size or the sort never counts again. Every mutation that adds, removes or edits a row calls `invalidatePagedList(queryClient, trpc.users)` (`src/lib/invalidate-paged-list.ts`), which invalidates `list` and `count` together.
- Pages read `searchParams` and prefetch `list.queryOptions(...)` and `count.queryOptions(...)` with the parsed URL, so the dehydrated query key is the client's. `usePageParams()` (`src/hooks/use-page-params.ts`, documents) and `useUserListParams()` (`src/hooks/use-user-list-params.ts`, users) write the URL; typing in the search box replaces the history entry instead of adding one. `PagedList` (`src/components/table-pagination.tsx`) renders the three states of a paged table: no row at all, a page past the end with a button to the last page, or the rows with the pager.

## Migrations
- `{{runScript "db:generate"}}` — Drizzle Kit generates SQL from the schema into `packages/db/drizzle`.
- `{{runScript "db:migrate"}}` — applies migrations to the local D1 sqlite (`--persist-to .wrangler`).
- `{{runScript "local-setup"}}` — deletes the local D1 state (`.wrangler/v3/d1`, so every account created by sign-up is lost), then runs `db:generate`, `db:migrate`, `db:roles` (the default roles, see [Auth & RBAC](auth-rbac.md)), `cf-typegen` and `db:seed` with `--fixtures`; run it once after install to get a working local DB, and again only to start over from the seeded state.
- Remote: `{{runScript "db:migrate:remote"}}` applies migrations to the live D1 database, then `{{runScript "db:roles:remote"}}` writes the default roles (the migrations create the role tables empty, and an empty table refuses every role but `admin`).

`drizzle.config.ts` resolves the newest local sqlite under `.wrangler/v3/d1/miniflare-D1DatabaseObject` for local introspection, and switches to the `d1-http` driver (account id / database id / API token) when `NODE_ENV=production`.

## Seeding
{{> seed/blueprint}}
The script opens the local miniflare sqlite directly via `@libsql/client` + `drizzle-orm/libsql` (no Bun API: it runs under Node and Bun), creates `admin@example.com` / `manager@example.com` / `user@example.com` through `createAuth(db).api.signUpEmail` (assigning roles afterwards) and, with `--fixtures`, inserts 27 demo users (they sign in with the demo password) and 24 demo documents, a third of them already expired to exercise the cron purge: enough rows for the users table and the documents list to fill three pages.
